<h3>What &quot;Conversion&quot; Means for Contractors</h3>
<p>Before going further, let us clarify what we measured as &quot;conversion.&quot; For contractor websites, a conversion is any action that generates a lead:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Phone call</strong>: Clicking a click-to-call button or manually dialing the number displayed.</li>
<li><strong>Form submission</strong>: Completing a contact form, quote request, or appointment booking form.</li>
<li><strong>Chat initiation</strong>: Starting a live chat or AI chatbot conversation.</li>
<li><strong>Email click</strong>: Tapping a mailto link to send an email inquiry.</li>
</ol>
<p>Phone calls are the highest-intent conversion. A homeowner who calls is typically ready to book. Form submissions indicate strong interest but may require follow-up. Chat initiations vary in intent but represent an engaged visitor.</p>
<p>Our study measured all four conversion types and found that <strong>phone calls dominate mobile conversions at 62%</strong>, followed by form submissions at 28%, chat at 7%, and email at 3%. This underscores the critical importance of prominent, functional click-to-call buttons on mobile contractor websites.</p>
<h3>The Top 10% vs. The Bottom 50%</h3>
<p>The most inspiring finding in our conversion data is the performance gap between the best and worst mobile experiences. Contractors in the <strong>top 10% of mobile performance achieved mobile conversion rates of 5.1%</strong>, actually exceeding the average desktop conversion rate. Meanwhile, the <strong>bottom 50% of contractors averaged just 1.6% mobile conversion</strong>.</p>
<p>This 3.2x difference between top performers and the bottom half proves that mobile conversion is not inherently lower than desktop. It is lower because most contractors have poorly optimized mobile experiences. The contractors who invest in mobile optimization are capturing leads their competitors never see.</p>
<hr>
<h2>Mobile Speed vs. Conversion: Every Second Costs You Leads</h2>
<p>If there is one metric that predicts mobile lead generation success more than any other, it is <strong>page load speed</strong>. Our correlation analysis found a strong negative relationship between mobile load time and mobile conversion rate. Simply put: the slower your site loads on a phone, the fewer leads you generate.</p>
<h3>The Speed-to-Conversion Curve</h3>
<p>We segmented the 100 contractor websites by their mobile Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), a Core Web Vital that measures how long the main content takes to load. Here is what we found:</p>
